export const CLEANJIBE_KNOWLEDGE = "# CleanJibe — what you know\n\n# CleanJibe — what Ask CleanJibe knows\n\nGENERATED by web/tools/make_assistant_knowledge.py from docs/copy. Do not edit.\n\nCleanJibe analyses wingfoil sessions: when you were up on the foil, every flight, and a verdict on every turn (flew through, touchdown or fell in, and clean). It runs as an iPhone app, in the browser at cleanjibe.org, and as a Garmin watch app that judges live on the wrist. It is free and non-commercial, built by riders.\n\nAnswering rules: use only facts from this file and from the state you are given. Never estimate a number yourself. A number marked estimated stays estimated. If the answer is not here, say you do not know and point to cleanjibe.org/beta/#tell. Answer in the language of the question. Keep the rider vocabulary below exactly. A session's speeds come in knots (the fields ending in Kn); say every speed in the rider's unit from units.speed, converted (1 kn = 1.852 km/h = 1.151 mph = 0.514 m/s), and never give the knots beside it unless asked. The help's thresholds are written in km/h; convert those to the rider's unit too. The state names no place: never say where a session was ridden. The example session below (Lake Garda) is the app's demo, never the rider's. There is no Android app; on Android, CleanJibe is the browser app at cleanjibe.org/app. The vocabulary rule holds in every language: never \"success\", \"successful\" or \"carried\", nor their translations (German \"erfolgreich\", \"Erfolg\"); say flew through, clean, dry, touchdown or fell in.\n\n## How CleanJibe talks (docs/voice.md)\n\n1. **One thought per sentence.** About 12 words; never more than 20. Two \"and\"s or a pair of\n   dashes is two sentences.\n2. **The rider first, the mechanics after.** *Your session shows up in CleanJibe by\n   itself.* Then, where it helps: *intervals.icu carries it over, because Garmin has no open\n   API.* A sentence about the mechanics is allowed when a rider can do something with it:\n   choose a tool, fix a setup, understand a verdict. It is cut when it only describes what\n   the software did.\n3. **What, then why — and the why only if it changes what the rider does.** \"Restart the\n   watch once after installing.\" needs no reason. \"Sessions arrive by themselves\" needs one\n   clause: \"Garmin has no open API, so intervals.icu is the bridge.\"\n4. **No em-dash, no semicolon, no parenthesis in rider text.** A dash is a second sentence\n   hiding. A parenthesis is either needed (then a sentence) or not (then gone).\n5. **No framing, no pointe, no fragment.** Banned shapes: *the one thing…*, *the half only\n   you can do*, *exactly as…*, *which is the whole point*, *not X, but Y* as a flourish, *and\n   that is why…*, *…, nothing else.*, and a verbless fragment as a sentence (*Free.* *Once.*\n   *Once.*). A fragment is allowed as a heading or as the tagline under the wordmark only; the tagline is *Your WingFoil session, measured.* (Jan, 23 September 2026). Say the thing.\n6. **Rider vocabulary, exactly.** *flew through*, *touchdown*, *fell in*, *clean*, *dry\n   streak*, *on foil*. Never *carried*, *success*, *no-fall*, *swim rate*\n   (`docs/copy/phrases.json` → `lexicon`). Path notation for the app: *Settings → intervals.icu*.\n7. **Second person, present tense.** *You*, not *the rider*; *opens*, not *will open*. The app\n   is *CleanJibe* or *the app*. *We* is the team that builds it, used sparingly and where a\n   person is meant: *tell us what you saw*, *we read every mail*. Never *I*.\n8. **Numbers stay numbers.** *2 s*, *500 m*, *10 riders*, *42 watches*. No \"a handful\", no\n   \"about ten\" where the number is known.\n9. **Register 2 gets one line of energy, not a paragraph.** A question, an image, a promise\n   — then back to plain. Excitement is a single sentence that earns the next tap.\n10. **Every cut fact keeps a home.** If a sentence goes, its fact goes to `docs/`, the help,\n    or a store text, or it was not a fact.\n\n11. **Wingfoiler words yes, IT words no.** A wingfoiler's insider word is welcome and is\n    taught once in the glossary: *estimated record*, *alpha 500*, *dry streak*, *foil up*.\n    An IT word never reaches a rider: *route*, *door*, *class (b)*, *pipeline*, *re-derive*,\n    *digest*, *payload* stay in `docs/`. When in doubt: would another wingfoiler use the\n    word on the beach?\n\n## What the numbers mean (the glossary)\n\n- **On foil**: How much of your session you spent flying rather than just moving. Also printed as: Foil time, foil, foil time.\n- **Flights & touchdowns**: One takeoff starts a flight. A touchdown or a fall ends it. Both are counted. Also printed as: Flights, Longest flight, flights, longest flight.\n- **Turn verdicts**: Every turn gets one: flew through, touchdown, or fell in. Also printed as: flew · touchdown · fell in.\n- **Dry streak**: How many turns in a row you stayed out of the water, tacks and jibes alike. A fall anywhere ends the run. Also printed as: best streaks.\n- **Flew streak**: How many turns in a row you flew through, tacks and jibes alike. A touchdown or a fall anywhere ends it.\n- **CPH**: Clean jibes per hour. Every jibe that passes the three clean gates counts. Also printed as: CPH · clean jibes per hour.\n- **JPH**: Dry jibes per hour. Falling in more often cannot raise it. Also printed as: JPH · dry jibes per hour.\n- **TPH**: Dry turns per hour, tacks and jibes together. It takes the place of JPH once a session has tacks in it. Also printed as: TPH · dry turns per hour.\n- **falls / h**: How often you fell in, per hour. Every fall counts, in a turn or not. Also printed as: WPH.\n- **Speed records**: Your fastest 2 seconds, 10 seconds, 500 m and nautical mile. The speedsurfing world uses the same windows. Also printed as: Best 1 NM, Best 10 s, Best 100 m, Best 2 s, Best 250 m, Best 500 m, avg speed, best 1 NM, best 10 s, best 100 m, best 2 s, best 250 m, best 500 m.\n- **Best 5×10 s**: The mean of your best five separate 10-second runs. They may not overlap, so one lucky reach cannot carry it. Also printed as: 5×10 s.\n- **Alpha 500**: Your fastest 500 m that ends within 50 m of where it started. It contains a jibe, so it measures the turn as well as the speed.\n- **Flew through**: The turn kept the foil, from the sweep until you were flying again. Also printed as: flew.\n- **Clean**: You flew through and held 70 % of your entry speed. Then you rode 10 s with no touchdown, fall or second off the foil. Also printed as: Clean jibes, clean jibes.\n- **Speed kept**: The share of counted turns that held 70 % of their entry speed and never dropped off the foil.\n- **Dry**: You did not fall in. A touchdown still counts as dry.\n- **Touchdown**: Your board touched the water and you got going again without falling in. A stop under 5 s counts as a touchdown. Longer, and it counts as a fall. Also printed as: Touchdowns, touchdowns.\n- **Fell in**: Every time you ended up in the water, in a turn or in a straight line. Also printed as: Falls, falls, fell.\n- **Glide-out**: A flight that ended without a stop. You settled onto the board and rode on, so it is not a loss. Also printed as: Glide-outs, glide-outs.\n- **Aborted turn**: A turn you started and fell in before it was done. It counts as a turn that fell in. Also printed as: aborted.\n- **Takeoffs**: How many times you got up on the foil. One takeoff starts every flight. Also printed as: Got up, Planing starts, got up.\n- **Attempts**: Every pumping burst, whether you got up or not. Attempts are takeoffs plus failed attempts. Also printed as: Failed attempts, Takeoff attempts, failed attempts.\n\n## Help topics (the app's and the website's help, word for word)\n\n### Start here\n\n#### Getting started\nYour first session on the water, and where to send what you find.\nRide with the watch you already have. Your session comes into CleanJibe, and every turn gets a verdict.\nOn a Garmin, ride as always, with Windsurf or any other watersport profile. Set up intervals.icu once in Settings → intervals.icu. It is free, takes about 5 minutes, and your old sessions come in too.\nWant more out there? The CleanJibe watch app buzzes each verdict and counts your pump strokes. Too small to read? Set Data screens to Large text.\n[iPhone app only] You can also open a .fit file straight from Files, Mail or a message. It can be yours, or one a friend sent you.\nWe ride too. We built CleanJibe for the jibes we were chasing ourselves.\n- Garmin, as you ride today: Ride as always, with Windsurf or any watersport profile. Connect intervals.icu once, and your old sessions come in too. The CleanJibe watch app adds verdicts on your wrist.\n- The CleanJibe Apple Watch app [only in: beta, dev] [iPhone app only]: Install it with the CleanJibe beta and record on your wrist. The watch only records, and the iPhone does the analysis.\n- Apple's own Workout app [only in: beta, dev] [iPhone app only]: No extra app on the watch: record a Surfing workout and import it from Apple Health.\n- Any watch that writes a .fit: Sync it through intervals.icu, or share the file into CleanJibe from Files, Mail, AirDrop or any share sheet.\n- Strava [iPhone app only]: Settings → Strava → Connect with Strava, then Import → Import from Strava…. Strava keeps your track but not your watch's speed, so records are estimated.\n- No wind today?: Record a 3 to 5 minute walk on the watch. It arrives in CleanJibe like a session would, so you know the whole way in works.\n- Tell us what you saw: Menu → Support & ideas opens a mail to us, with your app and watch details already in it. Your mail decides what we build next.\n\n#### Which watches work with CleanJibe\nEvery watch works, one way or another. Each row says what yours can show.\nEvery watch that records a GPS track works. A number your file cannot support is left blank, never guessed.\nTwo things set the rows apart. First, **measured speed**: your file holds the watch's own speed, so your speed records count. Without it, speed is worked out from positions and the records are marked estimated.\n**Pump strokes and takeoff attempts** need a wrist accelerometer recorded during the session. Only the CleanJibe watch apps record one.\n- Garmin, with the CleanJibe watch app: You get everything: each verdict buzzes, and pumps count live. Log wrist motion adds the phone's pump analysis but slows the sync. Data screens → Large text gives big numbers.\n- Garmin, with Garmin's own profile or another app: You get every verdict and flight, with measured speed records. Your old sessions come in through intervals.icu too.\n- Apple Watch, with the CleanJibe watch app: It is in the beta. You get measured speed, plus pump strokes and takeoff attempts from your wrist.\n- Apple Watch, with Apple's Workout app: Bring it in through Strava or intervals.icu. In the beta, Apple Health hands it over with measured speed.\n- Polar, Suunto, COROS and the rest: A FIT gives you measured speed records, through intervals.icu or shared in by hand. A .gpx carries positions only, so its speed is estimated.\n- Anything that ends up on Strava: You get every verdict and flight, with speed estimated from positions. Connect Strava and pick the sessions.\n- A phone in a pouch, or no watch at all: Your flights, turns and map, with speed estimated from positions. Record with any GPS app and share the file in.\n\n#### Look around with the example session\nOne real session ships with the app. Load it before you connect anything.\nA real wingfoil session on Lake Garda. Ten minutes, 2 flights, 10 jibes, 2.6 km and 68 % foil time. The track, the speed chart, the turn outcomes and the heart-rate cost are all filled in.\nIt is not your data. CleanJibe marks it EXAMPLE in the list and on its own page. It stays out of Records, Trends and your gear totals.\n[iPhone app only] Delete it with a swipe. You can load it again from here.\nWe recorded it at Nago-Torbole, Lake Garda with the CleanJibe watch app and removed everything that identifies the rider. The rest of the recording is all there.\nThat includes the wrist accelerometer, so you see its pump strokes and failed takeoff attempts.\n\n### Record a session\n\n#### Recording with the CleanJibe Apple Watch app [only in: beta, dev] [iPhone app only]\nRecord on your Apple Watch. The session comes to the phone by itself.\nThe watch app records the GPS track, your heart rate and the wrist accelerometer at 50 Hz. Start it on the watch, ride, and end the workout. The session moves to the phone while both are in range.\nBecause the wrist is recorded, pump strokes and failed takeoff attempts are analysed on the phone. The speed is the watch's own, so the records are measured.\n- The wrist may go under: When your wrist goes under, the watch's pressure sensor notices, and that counts as a fall. The gap in the GPS track is marked, not filled in.\n- Let the workout finish: The watch sends the session once you end the workout. If it is not in the list yet, look again in a minute.\n\n#### Recording with the Apple Workout app [only in: beta, dev] [iPhone app only]\nNo Garmin, no extra app: record on your Apple Watch and import from Health.\nRecord a session with Apple's own Workout app and analyse it here. You need no Garmin, no account and no cable.\nHealth has no wingfoil workout type. CleanJibe reads the one you picked as a wingfoil session, because you asked it to.\nOpen Import → Apple Health, allow CleanJibe to read workouts, and pick the ones you want. Switch on \"Import new Health workouts automatically\". The next session is then waiting when you open the app.\nSpeed comes off the watch's own GPS receiver, so these speed records are measured. Nothing records your wrist accelerometer, so there are no pump strokes and no failed takeoff attempts.\n- Pick Surfing or Water Sports: Both are on by default, and Surfing is what CleanJibe's own watch app writes. Sailing works too. Switch it on in Import → Apple Health.\n- Let the workout finish saving: The route is written to Health when you end the workout. The watch may take a minute to hand it over.\n- Nothing is uploaded: CleanJibe reads the route and the heart rate of the workouts you pick, and analyses them on your phone. It never reads anything else in Health.\n\n#### Recording with a phone only\nRide with a tracker app on a phone in a pouch. It comes in through Strava or as a file.\nA phone records a GPS track as well as most watches do. CleanJibe reads it the same way.\nThe Import screen calls it positions only, because the speed is worked out from the positions.\nFoil time, flights, every turn verdict and clean jibe, the wind axis. Speed records are estimated from positions and marked so, and there are no pump strokes or takeoff attempts.\nKeep the phone dry, still and facing the sky. A waterproof pouch on the upper arm or high on the chest works.\nA pocket at hip height spends half the session underwater. Start the recording on the beach.\n- Strava, the way in that needs no file [iPhone app only]: Record in the Strava app and connect Strava here. Strava's phone app cannot export a file, so the import reads the activity out of your account instead.\n- Any GPS-logging app that writes a file: Anything on your phone that records a track and writes a .fit or a .gpx works. Save the track, tap Share, pick CleanJibe. Or open it from Files.\n- Which format, if you are offered a choice: Pick FIT. CleanJibe reads a .fit in every build, and a file with the watch's own speed gives measured records. A .gpx or a .tcx opens in the CleanJibe beta.\n\n### Bring it in\n\n#### Get set up with intervals.icu\nSet it up once, in 4 steps and about 5 minutes.\nYour Garmin sessions come in by themselves, your old ones too. The easiest way is intervals.icu, because Garmin has no open API. You can also open a .fit by hand, from Files, Mail or a Garmin export ZIP.\nNothing is uploaded, and nothing changes in either account. CleanJibe downloads the original recording of each watersport session and analyses it on your phone.\nPolar, Suunto and COROS work this way too. Their own apps sync to intervals.icu, and CleanJibe syncs from there.\nThe file your watch left there decides how much you get. A FIT gives you the full analysis. A GPX, or a TCX without speed, gives estimated speed records.\n- 1. Create a free intervals.icu account: Open intervals.icu and sign up. It is free, and the Google, Strava or e-mail account you already have will do.\n- 2. Connect Garmin in intervals.icu: In intervals.icu, Settings → device connections: connect your Garmin account. Your history back-fills in a few minutes, and every new session arrives on its own.\n- 3. Generate your personal API key: Still in intervals.icu: Settings → Developer Settings → API Key. Copy it. Developer settings are free for every user. No subscription is needed.\n- 4. Paste the key into CleanJibe: In CleanJibe, open Settings → intervals.icu and paste the key. Tap Save & check. CleanJibe checks it and says how many activities it can see.\n- Why Garmin says Windsurf: Garmin has no wingfoil profile. Garmin and Strava call the session Windsurf. CleanJibe calls it Wingfoil.\n- Where Garmin Connect shows the jibes: Under Connect IQ on the activity page: foil time, flights, the best 2 seconds, jibes and tacks.\n- When the jibes and tacks show up: They appear after a few minutes of flying, once the watch has worked out the wind. Set it by hand to have them from the start.\n- Why the Runs card stays empty: Runs belong to Garmin's own windsurf profile. No Connect IQ app can fill that card. CleanJibe's flights are the runs.\n\n#### Sessions straight from your Garmin watch [only in: beta, dev] [iPhone app only]\nThe CleanJibe watch app sends your session to your phone over Bluetooth, before intervals.icu has it.\nWhen you save, your watch sends a summary to your phone at once. The whole recording follows, page by page, through the Garmin Connect app.\nKeep CleanJibe open on your phone for about twenty seconds after you save. The watch buzzes once and shows phone ok when it is all across.\nYou get the full analysis: foil time, flights, every turn verdict and measured speed records.\nThe FIT still brings the laps. When it arrives through intervals.icu, it takes the session over. The name you gave it, your gear and your notes stay.\n- Switch it on, on the watch: In Garmin Connect, open the CleanJibe app's settings and switch on Send summary to phone app.\n- Choose your watch, on the phone: Settings → Garmin watch → Choose your watch. Pick it in Garmin Connect, which hands you back here.\n- What part of it means: The transfer stopped before the end, and nothing more came for a day. CleanJibe keeps what arrived and marks the session part of it. The FIT replaces it in full.\n- Walked away too soon: The watch keeps what it has not sent yet. Start the watch app near your phone, and it tries again.\n- Where it goes: From your watch to your phone, through the Garmin Connect app. It goes to no server on the way.\n\n#### Import from Strava [iPhone app only]\nConnect once, then pick the sessions you want. Strava keeps your track but not your watch's speed.\nCleanJibe lists your Strava sessions and imports the ones you pick. It only reads. It never writes, renames or posts to your account.\nA Strava session still shows foil time, flights, every turn verdict, the wind axis and the map.\nStrava keeps your track but not your watch's own speed, so the speed records are marked estimated. Without the watch's own speed, a fall can read as a touchdown. Your wrist was not recorded, so there are no pump strokes.\nIf the same afternoon is also on intervals.icu, import it from there instead. That is the original file off your watch, so those records are measured. Importing both is harmless, because the same session is never added twice.\n- Connect: Settings → Strava → Connect with Strava. Leave the private-activities box ticked or your \"Only you\" sessions will be missing.\n- Pick, or take everything new: Tap the sessions you want, or use Import all new. Anything already in your library is marked and cannot be picked twice.\n- Which activities are offered: Windsurf, Kitesurf, Surf, Workout and Stand-up paddling by default. Sail can be switched on. Anything named wing, foil, kite, surf or SUP is offered too.\n- Keep it automatic: Import from Strava… has a switch, Import new Strava activities automatically. With it on, CleanJibe checks Strava whenever you open the app.\n- A long history takes its time: Strava lets an app ask 200 times every 15 minutes. A first import of many seasons may ask you to come back later.\n- If connecting is refused: Strava lets a new app connect a limited number of riders. That says nothing about your account. Tell us through Menu → Support & ideas.\n- Strava full? Take the file: On a computer, open the activity on strava.com and choose ⋯ → Export Original. AirDrop it to the iPhone, then Import → FIT or ZIP….\n- Disconnecting: Settings → Strava → Disconnect Strava. The sessions you already imported stay in your library. They are yours now, analysed on this phone.\n\n#### Share from your watch app straight into CleanJibe\nPolar, Suunto and COROS can hand a session to CleanJibe as a file. Garmin's phone app cannot.\nEvery watch app can export a recording as a file, and CleanJibe reads .fit files. Export the session as a FIT and pick CleanJibe from the share sheet.\nIf it is not in the row, Save to Files and open it from there.\nIf you are asked for a format, pick FIT. A FIT carries the watch's own speed, so its records are measured.\nA .gpx or a .tcx carries positions only, so its records are marked estimated. Those two formats open in the CleanJibe beta.\nGarmin Connect's phone app has no export at all. Garmin owners have two better ways in: intervals.icu, or connect.garmin.com on a computer.\n- Garmin: No phone export. On a computer: connect.garmin.com → Activities → the activity → gear icon → Export File.\n- Suunto: On the phone: Calendar → tap the workout → ⋯ top right → FIT. Then Save to Files, or pick CleanJibe from the share sheet.\n- COROS: On the phone: Activities → tap the activity → ⋯ top right → Export → FIT. Older versions call it Export Data. Then use the share sheet.\n- Polar: Not on the phone. On flow.polar.com: Diary → click the session → Export → FIT. A computer is the reliable way.\n- Anything else [iPhone app only]: If an app can produce a FIT, CleanJibe reads it. Take it from AirDrop, Mail or Files. A ZIP of recordings works too: Import → FIT or ZIP….\n\n#### CleanJibe in a browser\nThe same analysis in any browser. Nothing to install, no account.\nOpen cleanjibe.org/app and drop a recording in. You get the session back with its flights, its turns and its speed records.\nIt keeps a library, records and trends of its own. Any phone with a browser can read a session this way.\nThe file is read inside the tab. Nothing is uploaded and no account is asked for.\n- What it reads: It reads a .fit, a .gpx or a .tcx from any watch. It has no beta, so every rider gets the same version.\n- From a Garmin: Garmin Connect's phone app cannot export. Paste your intervals.icu key into the browser app's Settings instead, or export the file at connect.garmin.com on a computer.\n- Install it from the browser: If your browser offers it, install the page. It gets an icon on your home screen, opens like an app and works with no signal.\n- Into the share sheet: Once it is installed, hold a file on the phone and pick Share, then CleanJibe. The analysis opens on it.\n\n#### Notifications for new sessions [iPhone app only]\nHear about a session while the phone is idle, and what decides whether you do.\nWhile the phone is idle, CleanJibe asks intervals.icu for new activity. It looks for windsurf, wing, kite, sail, surf and SUP, from any watch that syncs there.\nYou hear about the ones that are not in your library yet. The session is downloaded and analysed in the background, so tapping the notification usually opens a finished analysis.\niOS decides when a background app may run. It learns your habits and may hold a check back for hours. It never runs in Low Power Mode.\nIt never runs while Background App Refresh is off, under Settings → General → Background App Refresh. Pull down on Sessions to sync now.\n- It needs a key: CleanJibe looks in your intervals.icu account, so add the API key first, in Settings → intervals.icu.\n- Off by default: Turning it on is what asks iOS for permission. CleanJibe offers it once by itself, right after your key works.\n\n### Read the numbers\n\n#### What the numbers mean\nEvery word CleanJibe counts with, and where each one shows.\nEach number has one word, on the watch, the phone, the card and the site. If you see one number called two things, tell us.\n- On foil: How much of your session you spent flying rather than just moving. Also on the watch and in Garmin Connect.\n- Flights & touchdowns: One takeoff starts a flight. A touchdown or a fall ends it. Both are counted. Also on the watch and in Garmin Connect.\n- Turn verdicts: Every turn gets one: flew through, touchdown, or fell in. Also on the watch.\n- Dry streak: How many turns in a row you stayed out of the water, tacks and jibes alike. A fall anywhere ends the run. Also on the watch.\n- Flew streak: How many turns in a row you flew through, tacks and jibes alike. A touchdown or a fall anywhere ends it.\n- CPH: Clean jibes per hour. Every jibe that passes the three clean gates counts. Also on the watch.\n- JPH: Dry jibes per hour. Falling in more often cannot raise it.\n- TPH: Dry turns per hour, tacks and jibes together. It takes the place of JPH once a session has tacks in it.\n- falls / h: How often you fell in, per hour. Every fall counts, in a turn or not.\n- Speed records: Your fastest 2 seconds, 10 seconds, 500 m and nautical mile. The speedsurfing world uses the same windows. Also on the watch and in Garmin Connect.\n- Best 5×10 s: The mean of your best five separate 10-second runs. They may not overlap, so one lucky reach cannot carry it.\n- Alpha 500: Your fastest 500 m that ends within 50 m of where it started. It contains a jibe, so it measures the turn as well as the speed.\n- Flew through: The turn kept the foil, from the sweep until you were flying again. Also on the watch.\n- Clean: You flew through and held 70 % of your entry speed. Then you rode 10 s with no touchdown, fall or second off the foil. Also on the watch.\n- Speed kept: The share of counted turns that held 70 % of their entry speed and never dropped off the foil. Also on the watch and in Garmin Connect.\n- Dry: You did not fall in. A touchdown still counts as dry.\n- Touchdown: Your board touched the water and you got going again without falling in. Also on the watch.\n- Touchdown or fall: A stop under 5 s counts as a touchdown. Longer, and it counts as a fall.\n- Fell in: Every time you ended up in the water, in a turn or in a straight line. Also on the watch.\n- Glide-out: A flight that ended without a stop. You settled onto the board and rode on, so it is not a loss.\n- Aborted turn: A turn you started and fell in before it was done. It counts as a turn that fell in.\n- Takeoffs: How many times you got up on the foil. One takeoff starts every flight. Also on the watch.\n- Attempts: Every pumping burst, whether you got up or not. Attempts are takeoffs plus failed attempts. Also on the watch.\n\n#### Reading the map\nWhat the chips, the colours, the arrows and the dots mean.\nEvery chip under the map is a switch. Tap one to hide that kind of mark on the map and in the speed chart at once.\nA hidden chip stays in place, struck through, and \"show all\" brings everything back. A chip this session has nothing for cannot be switched. The three rows are the track, the marks on it, and the map's own controls.\nTap the track to move the replay playhead. Tap a mark, or a flown stretch, for its own facts.\nIn the iPhone app, one finger on the map scrolls the page. Use two fingers to move or zoom the map.\n- The track: Tinted by phase: teal where you were flying, grey where you were not. Small chevrons along it point the way you were riding.\n- The dots: Verdicts on one ladder: green flew through, orange touchdown, red fell in. Grey is a course change and no verdict at all.\n- Solid or hollow: The fill tells two kinds of mark apart without a second colour. Solid is a turn's outcome. Hollow is a flight that ended on a straight line, outside any turn.\n- A star: A clean jibe, in a green of its own, taking the place of that jibe's dot. It needs both its chips: hide the outcome and the star goes with it.\n- Arrows, not dots: Takeoffs: an up-arrow got you up, a red u-turn is an attempt that did not. One chip hides both halves.\n- Bands and drops: The indigo bands are pump bursts. The cyan drop is the barometer seeing your wrist go under.\n\n#### Flights and foil time\nHow often you got up, how long you stayed up, and how far you went.\nA flight is every stretch you stayed up on the foil. It starts once you hold 12 km/h for 2 s. It ends when you drop under 8 km/h for 3 s.\nA flight is dated back to the first moment that counted, at both ends. Anything under 5 s is not a flight.\nA touchdown or a fall ends a flight. The next takeoff starts a new one.\nTaxiing, time in the water and the drift upwind count against your time on foil. A gap in the recording does not.\n- Foil time and On foil: Foil time is the minutes you flew. On foil is their share of timer time, which leaves out any stretch where the recording stopped or lost GPS.\n- Flights: How many separate times you got up and stayed up.\n- Longest flight: Your longest flight by time, with its distance underneath. Many short flights and one long one can give the same On foil.\n- Distance: How far you went, flying, taxiing and drifting. It adds up the watch's measured speed rather than positions, because position noise inflates a distance at low speed.\n\n#### Jumps [only in: beta, dev]\nHow many times you jumped, and an estimate of the highest.\nThe motion sensor on your wrist feels each jump. It notices the pop off the water, the time in the air and the landing. A jump counts only on the foil, at speed and outside a turn.\nThe height is worked out from that motion, so it is always marked estimated. No jump, no card.\n- Where it shows: On the Flights tab of a wingfoil session. It needs your wrist's motion: the CleanJibe Apple Watch app, or the Garmin app with Log wrist motion on.\n- On the Garmin watch: The beta counts your jumps live. Each one buzzes and shows JUMP with your count.\n\n#### Speed records\nYour fastest 2 seconds, 10 seconds, 500 m and nautical mile. The speedsurfing world uses the same windows.\nThe set is fixed, so your numbers compare with anyone's. It holds the four windows below, 100 m, 250 m and your best hour.\nBest 5×10 s and Alpha 500 are defined in What the numbers mean.\nAll of them use the watch's Doppler speed, measured to the exact edge of each window. So a watch that records once a second scores the same as one that records four times. No slow stretch is thrown away.\nA window never spans a recording gap. Tap a record card to see where on the track and on the speed trace it happened.\nA record counts as measured only when the recording holds the receiver's own speed channel. The rest are marked estimated.\n- Best 2 s: Your peak speed, averaged over 2 seconds. A single-sample maximum is noise. 2 seconds is a real burst, and the number most riders compare.\n- Best 10 s: The fastest 10-second run, a burst you had to hold. Luck and a single gust cannot carry it, so it usually sits 1 to 3 knots below your 2 s.\n- Best 500 m: Your fastest half-kilometre, measured along the line you actually rode. A curved run still counts, and cutting the corner flatters nothing.\n- Best 1 NM: Your fastest nautical mile, 1852 m. On most spots it needs more than one leg, so it measures how well you keep speed through your turns.\n- Knots or km/h: Knots by default. Settings → Units switches every speed on the phone to km/h.\n- The other three readers: The watch app has its own switch, under Garmin Connect → CleanJibe → Settings. Garmin Connect follows its own. Strava shows windsurf in knots.\n- A friend's session: Sessions a friend sent you stay out of your records, trends and totals.\n- GP3S and Doppler: GP3S is the rule set speedsurfers use for these windows. Doppler speed is what your watch measures from the satellite signal itself, not from positions.\n- \"Estimated\": A recording with positions but no speed channel has its speed worked out from them, which reads high. Every GPX is one, and some converted exports. It is marked everywhere.\n\n#### Measured and estimated speed records\nChoose whether a record from a track without measured speed counts.\nA measured record comes off a recording that carries your watch's own Doppler speed. An estimated one is worked out from positions, which reads high.\nOnly measured keeps estimated records out of your all-time table, your trends and your cards. The session that set one still shows it, marked.\nPrefer measured is the default. A measured record wins its row, and an estimated one fills a row no measured record has reached.\nInclude estimated counts every record and marks the estimated ones. Settings → Speed records is where you choose.\n\n#### The session story\nOne line over your numbers, and a chip on each record you beat.\nOver a session's key numbers, one line says what stood out. That can be your clean jibes, or a record you beat this month, this season or ever.\nEach record you beat gets a chip on its cell. With nothing to celebrate, the line is the plain tally.\n\n#### Tacks, jibes and course changes\nWhat counts as a turn, and what is just a change of direction.\nEvery jibe and tack you carve counts as a turn. To count, it needs 60° of heading change inside 8 s, on the foil or within 3 s of it.\nIt must also carve a real arc. That means at least 12 m of path, at a radius of 6 m or more. Spinning around beside the board carves no arc.\nWhat kind of turn it was depends on the wind axis. A tack or a jibe needs 90° of heading change and a crossing of the axis:\n- Jibe: Your course crosses the wind axis through downwind.\n- Tack: Your course crosses the wind axis through upwind.\n- Bear-away / round-up: A course change under 90°, or one that never crosses the axis. It stays out of the tack and jibe tallies.\n- Turn: A turn on a session where the wind axis was too uncertain to name it. It is still counted, just unnamed.\n- Most of my turns are: Your track gives the wind axis as a line. When a session cannot say which end the wind blew from, your habit does.\n- Changing it later [iPhone app only]: Sessions already in your library change only when you re-run the analysis, from Settings → Storage.\n- My tacks show as jibes [iPhone app only]: Clear a wrong wind on the watch with Wind → Unset, since it always wins. Set Settings → Analysis → Most of my turns are to Mostly tacks, then re-analyse.\n\n#### Turn outcomes: flew through, touchdown, fell in\nWhat actually happened to the foil in the turn.\nEvery turn ends one of three ways: flew through, touchdown, or fell in. CleanJibe watches from the turn start until you are flying again.\nFlying again means 2 s back above 70 % of your entry speed. The window closes after 12 s.\nA jibe exited at marginal speed can bleed off for 6 to 12 seconds before the foil stalls. That mush-out is the jibe's fault. A jibe you power out of closes its window in a second or two.\nInside that window, your speed is always read. So is the barometer, because a wrist under water looks like a huge drop in altitude. On a CleanJibe watch recording, the accelerometer is read too.\n- If you never get going again: The window follows you for up to 30 s instead of 12. The stop you end in is then this turn's.\n- Flew through: You never left the foil. No sample in the window is off-foil.\n- Touchdown: Your board touched the water and you got going again without falling in. A stop under 5 s counts as a touchdown. Longer, and it counts as a fall.\n- Fell in: You stopped for more than 5 s, or the barometer says your wrist went under.\n- Touchdowns on a straight line: A touchdown outside any turn is counted for the session. It ends the flight, like any touchdown.\n\n#### Clean jibes\nThe jibe you were working for: foil up the whole way, speed held, straight back to riding.\nThis is the jibe you were working for. A **clean** jibe passes three gates. You flew through, held your speed, then rode 10 s with no touchdown, fall or second off the foil.\n**Flew through** means you never lost the foil until you were flying again.\nHolding the speed means your slowest point stays at or above 70 % of your entry speed. You never drop below the foil exit speed.\nThose 10 s start when the turn ends. A wrist under water in them counts as a fall.\nEvery clean jibe also flew through. A jibe that flew through can still miss clean, if you lost speed or touched down coming out.\n- Jibes only: A tack has no clean reading to carry, so the Tacks card reports only how its tacks ended.\n- Score: The share of your entry speed you held through the turn, 0 to 100. It is the evidence behind \"clean\", printed beside every turn.\n\n#### Rates\nYour jibes and falls per hour on the water, and when a session is too short for one.\nA rate is a count per hour on the water. The hour is your timer time: the recording minus its pauses and gaps.\n**CPH** counts your clean jibes, the jibe you were working for.\n**JPH** counts your dry jibes: flew through or touchdown. Falling in more often never raises it.\nOnce a session has a tack, **TPH** takes its place and counts your dry turns.\n**falls / h** counts every fall, in a turn or not.\nUnder 20 minutes on the timer, a session shows no rate. Five clean jibes in ten minutes would read as 30 an hour. Trends and records leave it out too.\nThe example session keeps its rates, so you can see them before your own.\n\n#### Port / starboard\nWhich tack you were on going in, and which side you avoid.\nYour entry tack is the tack you came into the turn on. It is not which way the board rotated.\nThe side is read from your wind angle before the turn. A 50/50 split means you work both sides equally. A lopsided split is the tack you quietly stop choosing, and usually the one worth practising.\nThe Trends screen plots this over time as \"% port\", with 50 % marked.\n\n#### Fell in\nEvery time you ended up in the water, in a turn or in a straight line.\nA fall means you stopped for more than 5 seconds, or the barometer caught your wrist going under.\nThe split matters. Falls in a turn are a turning problem. Falls in a straight line are a gust, a ventilation or a tip catching. Each fall is counted once. A fall inside a turn's window belongs to that turn.\nThe jibe tally is a different question. Its three counts are out of your jibes, so a fall in a straight line is not in them. This number counts the whole session.\n\n#### Glide-outs\nFlights that ended without ever stopping.\nThe flight ended and you kept moving. You settled onto the board and taxied on, or you chose to stop riding. No stop was ever measured, so this is not counted as a loss.\nWhen a flight ends because the recording stopped, CleanJibe calls it unknown. There is nothing there to judge, so it stays out of every tally.\n\n#### The Next session card [only in: beta, dev]\nOne thing to work on next time, from this session's turns.\nThe card sits at the top of the Turns tab. It names the tip that came up most often, and on how many of your turns.\nIt also compares your port and starboard entries and names the side to work on. It does that only with at least 3 turns on each side.\n\n#### Attempts & getting up\nHow often you pumped, including the times you did not get up.\nAttempts are takeoffs plus failed attempts. A pumping burst is a failed attempt when no flight starts within 10 seconds of your last stroke. Bursts closer together than that are chained into one attempt.\nTakeoffs alone cannot show this. Getting up 20 times out of 22 looks like getting up 20 out of 40.\nIt needs the wrist accelerometer, which only the CleanJibe watch app records. Without it your failures are invisible, so the share is shown as unknown rather than a flattering 100 %.\n\n#### Pumps to takeoff\nHow many strokes each flight cost you.\nEvery flight shows how many pump strokes it took to get up. The count starts at the rising speed before the flight, or at the pump burst that led into it.\nUnder 3 strokes is a free takeoff: the wind got you up alone. Free takeoffs say more about the conditions than about you. They stay out of the averages and are shown on their own.\nWhen the recording cut a run short, its cost is unknown. The flight still counts as a takeoff that worked, but it stays out of the averages.\n\n#### Pump strokes\nEvery stroke in the session, and the ones you did in flight.\nStrokes are counted from the wrist accelerometer. Only the strength of each movement counts, so how your wrist was turned does not matter.\nIn-flight strokes hold or extend a glide rather than get you up. That is different work, so they are counted separately.\n\n#### Heart rate: cost and coverage\nHow hard each takeoff hit you, and when to trust the number.\nHeart-rate cost shows how hard each takeoff hit you. It is how far your pulse climbed from just before the pumping to its peak.\nYour baseline is the median of the 10 s before the takeoff run. The peak is searched 30 s forward, because a wrist sensor trails effort by 10 to 20 s.\nA negative cost means you were still recovering when you started. That is not the same as a takeoff that cost nothing.\nThe card does not appear when your recording has no usable heart rate. Nothing is estimated while the sensor was silent.\n- Coverage: The share of a window with good readings, between 30 and 220 bpm and no more than 10 seconds apart. Below 60 % no number is shown.\n- Why it drops out: A wrist sensor under a wetsuit sleeve in cold water drops out and sticks. Then you see no number rather than a guess.\n- The fatigue chart: Your session in 20-minute blocks, each showing what its takeoffs cost, with the share of attempts that got up underneath. An empty block is shaded, not drawn as zero.\n- Read the cost bars with the baseline note: A rise measured against a baseline that has drifted upward gets smaller as you tire. A shrinking late cost is not evidence that the takeoffs got easier.\n\n#### Wind axis & confidence\nThe wind direction worked out from how you actually rode.\nYour turns become tacks and jibes because CleanJibe knows the wind. It works the wind out from your own track, with no weather station.\nIt finds your two main reaching directions in the headings you flew. The wind axis is the line halfway between them.\nThat gives an axis but not a side. The no-go zone breaks the tie. Of the two ends, the wind came from the one you rode almost nothing within ±45° of.\nConfidence combines how cleanly the two reaches separate with how decisive the no-go zone was. Below 50 % the axis is still shown, but your turns stay unnamed \"turns\" rather than tacks and jibes.\nA wind direction you set on the watch always wins.\n\n#### Windsurf (experimental) [only in: dev]\nThe same engine without the wing. The planing speeds are a guess.\nA session can be analysed as **Wingfoil**, **Windsurf foil** or **Windsurf fin**. The row is on the session's Details tab, under \"Analyse as\". Changing it works that one session out again, and nothing else.\nNone of Garmin, Strava, intervals.icu and Apple Health has a wingfoil sport. Most riders record under the windsurf profile. A new session cannot say which rig it was ridden on.\nSo CleanJibe reads it as whatever you set under Settings → \"I mostly ride\". It is marked with a **?** until you have looked, and listed after each import. Sessions from the CleanJibe watch app are never asked about.\n- What works: Jibes and tacks are the same detector, the same thresholds and the same three verdicts. So are the speed records, the wind axis, the wrist-under marks and the map.\n- What is off: Pumping. With no wing to load, pump chips, stroke counts, pumps-to-takeoff and the heart-rate cost are **absent rather than zero**. A takeoff becomes a planing start.\n- Windsurf foil: The wingfoil reading with pumping switched off. Every speed and every threshold is the same, because a foil flies the same way under either rig.\n- Windsurf fin: Also moves the two speeds that decide when you are going. 20 km/h starts planing and 15 km/h stops it, against 12 and 8 on a foil.\n- Those two speeds are provisional: They are a first guess, because we have no real fin sessions yet. If your planing time looks wrong, tell us that number.\n- In the library: \"Foil time\" means planing time here, and \"lost the foil\" means stopped planing. Windsurf sessions count towards your trends. There is no separate record set.\n\n### Share\n\n#### Share cards\nOne picture of a session, made to post.\nAny session becomes a card. It holds the track, the numbers that matter, and where the analysis came from. Pick a shape and the big number: clean jibes, best 2 s or tacks. Your own photo can go behind it.\nOr pick the map as the background and the track is drawn over the water you rode. That one needs a connection. Without one the card comes out plain.\nA trip, a month or a season makes a card too. Draw all its tracks on one another, one session big, or a collage of them.\nThe card is made on your phone and goes nowhere until you send it.\n\n#### Replay clips [iPhone app only]\nRecord the replay as a video.\nThe replay plays a session back on its own track, with a commentary that follows what is happening. Scrub to the part worth watching, then record it as a video.\nAsk for a 10, 25 or 60-second clip. The app sets the playback speed to fit it. Or take \"full detail\" and let the session run as long as it runs.\nThe frame is yours too. Take 9:16 for a story, 1:1 for a post, 16:9 for a chat, or the whole screen. Photos from that afternoon can be spliced in.\nYou can lay your own music under it, trimmed or looped and faded at both ends. Nothing is uploaded: the video is rendered on the phone.\n\n#### Sending a session to a friend\nShare the original recording, stripped of anything identifying.\nYou can share the original .fit file of any session. The watch serial, your rider profile and your lifetime totals are removed first. The ride itself is untouched, so the analysis your friend gets is identical to yours.\nThey can open it in CleanJibe, or drop it into the free browser app at cleanjibe.org without installing anything.\n\n#### The session video [only in: beta, dev]\nYour session as a short film, made on this phone.\nOpen a session, tap Share, then Export video. Pick a length and, if you like, music you may share.\nA photo you took during the session drops in at the moment you took it.\n- Testing it?: Keep the 20 s length, wait for the bar to finish, then share the clip.\n\n### Your library\n\n#### Backing up your library\nA new phone carries everything by itself. A fresh start needs a file.\n[iPhone app only] Setting up a new iPhone from this one carries your library across, and so does an iCloud backup.\n[iPhone app only] Settings → Library backup is for the case neither covers. That is a phone set up as new, or the app deleted and installed again.\nThe file holds every recording you imported, and what nothing else can bring back. That is each session's name and caption, whose it was, its gear. Your spot names and the sessions you deleted on purpose are there too.\nRestoring never overwrites. Sessions already in your library keep their own analysis. Details you changed since are left alone. Restoring the same file twice does nothing the second time. Sessions you deleted after the backup stay deleted.\n- Save the file yourself [iPhone app only]: Back up library makes the file, and Save… puts it in Files, iCloud Drive or on a Mac. CleanJibe keeps no copy.\n- Your own recordings, nothing stripped: The .fit and .gpx files inside are the originals. Nothing is removed. A session you send a friend is stripped first. This file is for you, not for sharing.\n- How big it will be: A session recorded with the CleanJibe watch app carries a 100 Hz accelerometer recording, about 95 % of its size. The app estimates the total before it starts.\n- A newer backup: CleanJibe will not open a backup from a newer version of itself. Update the app and try again. An older backup is brought up to date.\n- Leaving the beta: When the App Store version is out, back up in the beta first, then install it. If the beta updated your library, a later App Store update opens that backup.\nIn the browser: In the browser app, Settings → Your data is the backup. Download all saves one zip, and Restore from a backup reads it back.\n\n#### Sessions someone else rode\nA friend's session is shown in full but kept out of your records.\nWhen you import a file, CleanJibe asks whose session it is. A friend's session is saved and shown in full: map, replay, every turn. It stays out of your records, trends and gear totals.\nTheir fast run never becomes your personal best. The name you give is stored on your phone only.\n\n#### Grouping and filtering the library [only in: beta, dev]\nGroup your sessions by month, year or spot, and narrow the list.\nThe control over the list groups your sessions: All, Month, Year or Spot.\nThe filter narrows the list to a spot or to dates. It narrows this list only, never your records.\n\n#### Widgets and the watch complication [only in: beta, dev]\nYour numbers on the home screen, and a one-tap start on the watch.\nThree widgets: Last session, This week, your time on the foil over seven days, and Personal bests. Add them from the home screen like any widget.\nOn the Apple Watch, the Start session complication starts a wingfoil recording in one tap.\n\n#### Adding sessions to Apple Health [only in: beta, dev]\nEach session can go to Apple Health as a workout.\nSwitch on Settings → Apple Health → Add sessions to Apple Health. Each session is then added as a Surfing workout.\nA session you imported from Health is left alone. Apple Health keeps the workout on your phone, and CleanJibe sends it nowhere.\n\n#### What leaves your phone\nThere is no account, and no session of yours is uploaded. The whole policy is on the web.\n[iPhone app only] There is no CleanJibe account, and no session you import is sent to a CleanJibe server. It is analysed on this phone and stays on it. The iPhone app has no advertising and no tracking.\n[iPhone app only] The app talks to four places, each only when you use it. Your own login goes to intervals.icu and Strava. Apple Maps loads while a map is on screen. To name a new spot, it sends one rounded position.\n- Your location [only in: beta, dev] [iPhone app only]: The Apple Watch app reads it while a workout you started is running. It goes to your own iPhone and nowhere else.\n- Usage counts [only in: beta, dev] [iPhone app only]: The beta counts which screens you open and which features work, with umami like the website. It sets no cookies and keeps no ID. It never sees a session.\n- A daily look for a newer build [only in: beta, dev]: Once a day the beta asks cleanjibe.org whether a newer build exists. It sends nothing about you.\n- The Garmin link [only in: beta, dev]: Your Garmin watch sends each session to this phone over Bluetooth, through the Garmin Connect app. It goes nowhere else.\n- Ask CleanJibe [only in: beta, dev]: Ask CleanJibe sends your question, and a session's numbers when you ask from one, to Anthropic's Claude. It is kept 30 days. An idea you share is kept a year for the team.\n- iCloud Drive [only in: dev]: Your library can live in your own iCloud Drive, so your iPhone and iPad share it. It is your iCloud, not a server of ours.\n- The Garmin watch map [only in: dev]: Where I am now asks for one position, to draw the map your Garmin watch shows.\n- Your intervals.icu key [iPhone app only]: Kept in this iPhone's Keychain, never copied to iCloud and never written to a log. It goes to intervals.icu itself, encrypted, and nowhere else.\n- Treat it like a password: It opens your intervals.icu account. Remove it in Settings → intervals.icu, or regenerate it in Developer Settings. The old key stops working at once.\n- What the website counts: cleanjibe.org and the browser app count page views and taps with umami. It sets no cookies. It never sees your sessions, your files or any number off a ride.\nIn the browser: In the browser app, a session is analysed in your browser and stays in its storage. Nothing is uploaded. Your intervals.icu key stays in this browser too. It goes to intervals.icu itself and nowhere else. Ask CleanJibe on the Help page sends a question only when you send one. It is kept 30 days, an idea for the team a year.\n\n#### Analysis engine version\nWhen the analysis improves, your old sessions are worked out again.\nThe footer of a session shows which version of the analysis engine produced its numbers.\nWhen a new version would give a session different numbers, CleanJibe works it out again. It reads that session's own archived recording, the next time you open it.\n[iPhone app only] Your original recording is never changed. Only the numbers are. Settings → Storage → Re-run analysis works them all out again at any time.\n\n### Something wrong?\n\n#### When the sync does not work\nThe four things that actually go wrong, and the fix for each.\nCleanJibe tells you what went wrong, because each problem has its own fix. A rejected key means your key. An empty list usually means Garmin is not connected yet. A network error is neither.\n- \"intervals.icu rejected the API key\": The key is wrong, or you made a new one after pasting it. Copy it again from intervals.icu, Settings → Developer Settings.\n- The sync succeeds but the list stays empty: Either Garmin is not connected in intervals.icu yet, or none of your activities is a watersport. CleanJibe takes Windsurf, Kitesurf, Sail, Surfing, SUP and anything named wing or foil.\n- \"Could not reach intervals.icu\": The connection dropped, and your key is fine. Nothing was lost or half-imported. Check your connection and sync again.\n- Older sessions are missing [iPhone app only]: The sync looks two years back and skips what the library already holds. For more, use Import → FIT or ZIP… with the Garmin export ZIP.\n\n#### The watch update does not arrive\nThe store queued it without installing it. Restart the watch, then sync.\nGarmin's Connect IQ store sometimes queues an update without installing it, most often on the fenix 5 Plus family.\nRestart the watch, then sync in Garmin Connect.\nIf it still shows the old version, install it through Garmin Express over the cable.\nWe update CleanJibe often, so you may meet this more than with other apps.\n\n#### When the watch and the phone show different numbers\nThis is normal, and the phone's number is the one to trust.\nA session from the CleanJibe watch app brings the summary the watch worked out while you rode. The watch does that in one pass, with very little memory. The phone does the whole job again, and compares the two.\nThe banner appears when foil time differs by more than 5 %. It appears for a speed record off by more than 0.3 knots. It appears for a flight, turn or attempt count off by more than one.\nThe phone's number is the one to go by. Nothing is wrong with your session. It only means the watch's live guess needs more work from us.\n\n#### Sending feedback\nThe app fills in build, phone and session. You write the sentence. Ideas and wishes are as welcome as bugs.\nOne mail carries all of it. Name a missing column, a word that reads wrong, or something you would rather the app did differently.\nMenu → Support & ideas opens the mail. So do \"Something off, or an idea? Send feedback\" at the foot of every page and \"Report a problem with this session…\" in the share sheet.\nThe mail asks three questions, with a blank line under each.\n[iPhone app only] Nothing leaves the phone until you tap Send. It is Apple's own composer, and it comes to us at info@cleanjibe.org. We read every mail. No part of CleanJibe sends anything by itself.\n- What is already in the mail [iPhone app only]: Under a line of dashes: app and engine version, tuned thresholds, your phone, iOS and locale. The paired watch is there, with how many sessions came in each way.\n- From a session: It also carries that session's date, spot and length, what recorded it and which app version read it. Its share card is attached.\n- Usage statistics: The sheet before the mail can add what this phone counted. That is which features you used, and whether they worked. Switch it off there, or delete any line.\n- Read it before you send [iPhone app only]: Every line is there to read and to edit. If this phone has no mail account, the app hands the same text to whatever you do use.\n- If you are on the beta [iPhone app only]: A screenshot taken inside the app offers Send Beta Feedback. That attaches the screenshot and the device logs. Take it for a crash.\n- An idea, told to Ask CleanJibe: Ask CleanJibe, on the Help page of the beta and of cleanjibe.org, passes an idea on to the team. The team reads every one.\n- About the watch app itself: Anything about the watch app can go through its Connect IQ store listing. For a number that looks wrong, the mail here is the one worth sending.\n\n#### Send a session to us [only in: beta, dev]\nMail us one session and your notes, so we can chase a wrong number.\nWe can only chase a wrong number on the recording that produced it. This puts the recording, your notes and the app's details into one mail.\nThe file holds your track, your heart rate and your times. We use it only to improve the analysis, and we never publish it.\nYou see the whole mail before it goes. Edit any line, delete any line, or close it and nothing is sent.\nOpen a session, tap Share, then Send this session to us.\n\n## Which feature is in which channel\n\nThe iPhone app comes in three channels from one build: release (App Store), beta (TestFlight, public link testflight.apple.com/join/nygqGGcn) and dev (a second app for a few hand-picked testers). Beta has everything below; dev has beta's features and the dev list. The website runs the release analysis.\n\nBeta adds:\n- The Garmin link: your watch sends a summary the moment you save, then the whole session.\n- .gpx and .tcx files, so a session exported from a Polar, a Suunto or a COROS opens straight from Files.\n- Apple Health, both ways: what Apple's Workout app recorded is read in, and your sessions are written back as workouts.\n- The CleanJibe Apple Watch app, which records on your wrist and hands the session to the phone.\n- Home-screen widgets and the watch complication.\n- The session video: your afternoon as a film rather than a card.\n- Grouping the library by month, year or spot, and filtering it.\n- Send a session and its recording to us from its Share page or a turn, with your notes.\n- Jumps: how many you jumped, and an estimate of the highest, from the watch's motion sensor.\n- Ask CleanJibe: a question in your own words, answered from the help and, on a session, from its numbers. Tell it your ideas, and the team reads them.\n- Planned: a live view on the Apple Watch. Foil state, flights, records and turn verdicts on the wrist, as the Garmin app shows them.\n\nDev adds:\n- Your spot's map and the wind direction, sent from the phone to your Garmin watch.\n- Windsurf, foil and fin, with thresholds of its own\n- The tuning page: every analysis threshold on a slider, tried against your own sessions\n- iPad\n\n## The turn coach (what the turn page says, with its tips)\n\n- Clean: you flew through and held at least {pct} % of your entry speed. Then you rode {quiet} s with no touchdown, fall or second off the foil.\n- no clear wind direction in your track\n- Flew through / touchdown / fell in is the outcome. It says how the turn ended. Held is how much of your entry speed you kept through it, 0 to 100 %.\n- {pct} % confident, can't tell tacks from jibes\n- You held {score} of your entry speed. The board did not come far enough past the wind axis. This one is not clean.\n- Clean, and you barely slowed. You held {score} of your entry speed all the way round.\n- You flew all the way through, and it cost you speed. {entry} in, {low} at the low point.\n- This one ended in the water. {entry} coming in, {low} at the low point.\n- You held {score} of your entry speed right round. It still ended in the water.\n- You flew through and barely slowed. You held {score} of your entry speed all the way round.\n- It was flying again straight away.\n- downwind point\n- middle of the turn\n- head-to-wind\n- {seconds} off the foil before it flew again.\n- {entry} in, {low} at the low point. You held {score} of your entry speed.\n- You pumped this one back out.\n- You pumped this one back out in {strokes}.\n- You rode the turn itself and held {score} of your entry speed. The foil went a few seconds later, so this one is not clean.\n- You rode the turn, then the foil dropped again on the way out. This one does not count as clean.\n- You held {score} of your entry speed through the turn. The barometer then saw your wrist go under. This one is not clean.\n- The speed went before the {mid}. You were down to {low} with the turn still to come.\n- You held it into the {mid}. The speed went on the way out, down to {low}.\n- The foil touched down before the {mid}. The speed was already at {low} going in.\n- The foil touched down on the way out. You held {entry} into the {mid} and lost it after.\n- The barometer saw your wrist go under here. The foil was gone for a moment. {entry} in, {low} at the low point.\n- Next time, carry the turn further past the wind axis before you settle.\n- Next time, come in with more speed.\n- Next time, come in faster, or keep the wing powered through the downwind point.\n- Next time, power the wing up as soon as you are on the new tack.\n- Next time, stay on the foil for {hold} after the turn, and it counts as clean.\n- a few seconds\n- Next time, stay low and steady on the way out.\n\n## The newest release notes\n\n### 1.0.1 (151, beta, 2026-10-22): Best turns first\n- The Turns list sorts by time or best first.\n- A turn's page swipes through the list as you see it, filtered and sorted.\n- A tack is compared with your best tack, a jibe with your best clean jibe.\n\n### 0.9.29 (None, beta, 2026-10-22): A new best stays up longer\n- A new best 2 s stays big on your wrist for 3 seconds.\n- A jibe you start slowly now counts as a jibe, not a course change.\n- No turn goes missing from the saved file.\n\n### 0.9.28 (None, beta, 2026-10-18): Sessions straight to the iPhone beta\n- Your watch can send each session straight to the CleanJibe beta app on your iPhone.\n- Turn on Send summary to phone app in Garmin Connect.\n\n### 1.0.1 (136, beta, 2026-10-17): Ask CleanJibe\n- Ask CleanJibe answers a question in your own words, from the help texts.\n- Asked from a session, it reads that session's numbers and never guesses one.\n- Help has its row at the top, and a session has its button in the bar.\n\n### 0.9.27 (None, beta, 2026-10-17): A jump never feels like a fall\n- A jump now buzzes long then short, so it never feels like a fall.\n- Your turn's verdict always buzzes, even right after a jump.\n- Longest flight and new best wait until your third flight.\n- The Turns page spells out flew and dry, port and stbd, where they fit.\n\n### 0.9.26 (None, beta, 2026-10-17): Resume restarts a paused session\n- Resume in the session menu now restarts a paused session.\n- After a GPS gap or a pause, the turn you were in is judged on what happened before it.\n- With Log wrist motion on, the transfer time to Garmin Connect is much longer.\n- The watch now asks once whether to keep it, and says so after the save.\n";
